  "query_preview": "..(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 320 PM EST THU NOV 19 2026\n\nOn Saturday, a coastal low is expected to develop off the Carolinas\nand move up the Eastern Seaboard. At the same time, a shortwave \ntrough over the Central Plains will propagate eastward towards the\nMississippi Valley. Eastern Kentucky will be sandwiched between \nthese two features under surface high pressure. As such, both warm\nand dry conditions are expected again Saturday afternoon and \nevening. Temperatures will top out in the 60s and 70s, which are \nabove normal for this time of year. Low RH values are still a \nconcern as the dry airmass contributes to dew point values in the \n20s and 30s during peak heating. Min RH is currently forecast in\nthe upper teens or around 30 percent range. This pattern also \nsupports ridge/valley temperature splits again for Saturday \nnight. Nighttime lows are expected to drop into the 40s for the \nridge tops and 30s for sheltered valley locations and low lying \nareas.\n\nThe aforementioned shortwave will move eastward towards the Ohio \nValley on Sunday. At the same time, a cold front will extend southward\nfrom the upper-level low and trek eastward throughout the day. \nWarm air advection will allow dew point values to climb into the \n40s to around 50 out ahead of the front. Combined with sufficient\nupper-level support, we will see our next opportunity for rain \nfrom late Sunday/Sunday evening as the cold front slides eastward\nacross the area. Given the fast moving front and narrow corridor \nof moisture (peak PWs of 0.8 to 1.0\" per the 00Z LREF), overall \nprecipitation amounts are expected to be light, a trace to a few\nhundredths of an inch. Behind the front, temperatures will drop \noff to below normal as we head into Monday. Daytime highs will \npeak in the 50s with nighttime lows dropping into the 30s. \n\nBeyond Monday, surface high pressure will build across the Ohio and \nTennessee Valleys. This will lead to rather tranquil weather for \nlate Monday through at least Tuesday. There is potential for \nridge/valley differences on Monday night or Tuesday night as high\npressure moves overhead along with the dry airmass pending the \ndegree of cloud cover. Daytime highs at this point are forecast to\nmax out below normal in the 40s with nighttime lows in the 30s. \nLater next week around the end of the period, guidance from \nECMWF/LREF indicate an active synoptic pattern setting up with a \ndeepening upper-level trough in the Great Lakes vicinity along \nwith a subtropical disturbance over the southeast or near the \nGulf Coast. This system could bring another chance for \nprecipitation from Wednesday into Thanksgiving. Models differ on \nthe strength and placement of these features, thus it is difficult\nto pinpoint specific impacts and timing at this juncture.",
